    <!-- Real Estate Coach Callout -->
    <div class="coach-callout">
      <h3>💡 Real Estate Coach's Take</h3>
      <p>
        Living near Puget Sound or regional lakes like Lake Washington, Lake Stevens, Green Lake, or Lake Ballinger is one of the best lifestyle perks in Western Washington. First, a quick reassurance: <strong>marine biotoxins and lake algae have zero impact on your home's indoor tap drinking water</strong>, which originates from protected, high-elevation municipal watersheds like the Cedar River or Spada Lake. However, for outdoor living, pets, and harvesting shellfish, staying informed is essential. Always follow the pet safety rule for lakes during warm late-summer months: <em>when in doubt, stay out</em>.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Educational Body Section -->
    <section class="narrative-section">
      <h2>Freshwater Cyanobacteria (Toxic Blue-Green Algae)</h2>
      <p>
        During warm, sunny late-summer months, elevated water temperatures combined with nutrient runoff can cause rapid growth of <strong>cyanobacteria (blue-green algae)</strong> in fresh water lakes.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Appearance:</strong> Toxic algae blooms often look like green pea soup, spilled green paint, or scum floating along the shoreline.</li>
        <li><strong>Toxigenic Risks:</strong> Cyanobacteria can produce potent nerve toxins (anatoxin-a) or liver toxins (microcystin). Dogs are particularly at risk because they swallow water while swimming or lick algae scum off their fur.</li>
        <li><strong>Testing & Monitoring:</strong> The Washington State Department of Ecology and local county health departments routinely collect water samples to test toxicity levels and issue swimming advisories.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Marine Biotoxins & Shellfish Harvesting Closures</h2>
      <p>
        Along Puget Sound saltwater beaches, microscopic marine algae can produce naturally occurring toxins that accumulate in filter-feeding shellfish like clams, oysters, and mussels:
      </p>

      <table class="comparison-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Marine Biotoxin Type</th>
            <th>Associated Illness</th>
            <th>Safety & Harvest Rule</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Paralytic Shellfish Poison (PSP)</strong></td>
            <td>Severe neurological illness.</td>
            <td>Cooking or freezing does NOT destroy toxins. Always check WA DOH beach map before harvesting.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Diarrhetic Shellfish Poison (DSP)</strong></td>
            <td>Gastrointestinal illness.</td>
            <td>Monitored regularly by state biologists along public recreational beaches.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Vibrio Bacteria</strong></td>
            <td>Bacterial infection.</td>
            <td>Thrives in warm summer mudflats. Keep harvested shellfish on ice immediately.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </section>

